.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt DTFL_SET_ALLOC_HOOKS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_set_alloc_hooks ,
.Nm dtfl_set_panic_hook
.Nd install libdtfl allocation and panic hooks
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/base.h
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_set_alloc_hooks "const dtfl_alloc_hooks *hooks"
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_set_panic_hook "dtfl_panic_fn fn" "void *ctx"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
All memory that libdtfl allocates comes from the allocation hooks.
.Fn dtfl_set_alloc_hooks
installs a
.Vt dtfl_alloc_hooks
structure whose
.Fa malloc_fn ,
.Fa realloc_fn
and
.Fa free_fn
members are called with the structure's
.Fa ctx .
It must be called before libdtfl allocates anything, and at most once.
Passing
.Dv NULL
restores the libc allocator, again only before first use.
The hooks must return memory aligned for libdtfl's own objects:
the alignment of
.Vt uint64_t ,
.Vt double ,
pointers and C11 atomics of those, which is 8 bytes on common 64-bit
platforms.
.Xr malloc 3
and PostgreSQL's
.Fn palloc
both qualify.
A user record or difference type that needs more
.Pq Vt long double , Vt __int128 , vector types
is stored in that memory, so it needs an allocator that aligns to
.Vt max_align_t .
.Pp
libdtfl never calls
.Fa realloc_fn
or
.Fa free_fn
with a
.Dv NULL
pointer: an empty buffer grows through
.Fa malloc_fn ,
and freeing
.Dv NULL
is skipped.
So an allocator whose reallocate and free functions need a live pointer,
such as PostgreSQL's
.Fn repalloc
and
.Fn pfree ,
can be hooked in directly.
A size of zero is passed as one.
Single requests can be large: the columns of a big arrangement grow
past a gigabyte, so a host allocator with a size cap (PostgreSQL's
.Fn palloc
stops at 1 GB) must use its unlimited form
.Pq Fn MemoryContextAllocHuge , Fn repalloc_huge .
.Pp
The panic hook is called when a caller violates a library contract,
for example by using a capability for the wrong output, and when an
allocation hook returns
.Dv NULL .
The hook receives a message and
.Fa ctx ,
and must not return: it may abort, exit, or
.Xr longjmp 3
out.
If it returns, libdtfl calls
.Xr abort 3 .
The default hook prints the message to standard error and aborts.
.Pp
A hook that
.Xr longjmp 3 Ns s
out leaves the worker that panicked in an unknown state: a structure
may be half updated, a reference count not yet dropped, or a mutex held.
Nothing reached from that worker may be used again, including
.Fn dtfl_worker_step
and
.Fn dtfl_worker_free .
Discard it by releasing its memory wholesale (for example by resetting
the host memory context the allocation hooks draw from), and build a
new worker if the computation is still needed.
The hooks are set once per process, so whatever
.Fa ctx
names must stay valid for the next worker: reset a context rather than
deleting it, or have
.Fa ctx
point at a variable the host updates.
With
.Fn dtfl_execute ,
the whole execution is lost, since its workers share state.
The process-wide hooks remain installed and valid.
Rust is the same: a panicking timely worker is not resumed.
.Fn dtfl_set_panic_hook
with a
.Dv NULL
.Fa fn
restores the default.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_set_alloc_hooks
returns 0 on success, or one of the errors below.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static void *my_malloc(size_t n, void *ctx) { return malloc(n); }
static void *my_realloc(void *p, size_t n, void *ctx) { return realloc(p, n); }
static void my_free(void *p, void *ctx) { free(p); }

dtfl_alloc_hooks h = { my_malloc, my_realloc, my_free, NULL };
if (dtfl_set_alloc_hooks(&h) != 0)
	errx(1, "%s", dtfl_last_error_detail());
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width DTFL_ESTATE
.It Bq Er DTFL_ESTATE
The library has already allocated memory.
.It Bq Er DTFL_EINVAL
A hook member is
.Dv NULL .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_set_thread_hooks 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_strerror 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7 ,
.Xr dtfl_embedding 7
.Sh HISTORY
Rust programs choose a global allocator at link time and abort on
allocation failure; these hooks are the C equivalent of both.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Both functions must be called before the first
.Fn dtfl_execute .
They are two of the four process-wide host hooks; the others install a
diagnostic log sink and thread-spawn callbacks.
See
.Xr dtfl_embedding 7 .
